{"nodes":[{"id":"211c2e4878d6155c","type":"group","x":-4300,"y":0,"width":2300,"height":3050,"label":"For students: surviving the current system","color":"4"},{"id":"564595ed6a6a7adb","type":"group","x":-1750,"y":0,"width":1550,"height":1150,"label":"Manifesto and scope","color":"2"},{"id":"535d2f9a91f72b34","type":"group","x":-50,"y":0,"width":1800,"height":1150,"label":"Core questions and assumptions","color":"3"},{"id":"c419feba97b58ac0","type":"group","x":1900,"y":0,"width":2200,"height":1750,"label":"What university currently does","color":"5"},{"id":"969e8ac4973afc88","type":"group","x":4300,"y":0,"width":2600,"height":2250,"label":"Principles for a better system","color":"4"},{"id":"6fcd5e6a8adbee24","type":"group","x":7200,"y":0,"width":1600,"height":1450,"label":"Education before university","color":"5"},{"id":"b93f1d2032022e8c","type":"group","x":-1750,"y":2200,"width":5850,"height":3150,"label":"Problems with current higher education","color":"1"},{"id":"b27c63784a299245","type":"group","x":4300,"y":2450,"width":2600,"height":2550,"label":"Research directions, examples, and possible experiments","color":"4"},{"id":"8afe1507c168c594","type":"group","x":7200,"y":1650,"width":3600,"height":3300,"label":"Bad teachers and teaching red flags","color":"1"},{"id":"ef5e865a210d4408","type":"group","x":-1750,"y":5600,"width":12550,"height":4700,"label":"External sources and research notes","color":"6"},{"id":"e7583f0bc8d97153","type":"group","x":-1600,"y":6050,"width":2200,"height":2350,"label":"Source notes: university, research, and lifelong learning","color":"6"},{"id":"21e529c44554a2bc","type":"group","x":700,"y":6050,"width":1800,"height":2350,"label":"Source notes: stress, passing, and curiosity","color":"6"},{"id":"b54f997f84054b76","type":"group","x":2600,"y":6050,"width":2100,"height":2350,"label":"Source notes: school redesign","color":"6"},{"id":"ff1ada2e348d815b","type":"group","x":4800,"y":6050,"width":2600,"height":2900,"label":"Source notes: purpose and problem-first learning","color":"6"},{"id":"426975240f484c70","type":"group","x":7500,"y":6050,"width":2200,"height":3200,"label":"Source notes: classical education critique","color":"6"},{"id":"1e34beddc6a1bd68","type":"group","x":9800,"y":6050,"width":900,"height":2050,"label":"Source notes: surveillance","color":"6"},{"id":"92bba0a4acbe273b","type":"group","x":-1600,"y":8600,"width":4200,"height":1500,"label":"Additional reading","color":"6"},{"id":"6a5da23545c39676","type":"text","x":-900,"y":100,"width":600,"height":294,"text":"## How to read this canvas\n\n1. Core questions about grades and knowledge\n2. The different jobs university currently does\n3. Problems in the current higher education system\n4. Principles for a better system\n5. Research directions, possible experiments, and examples\n6. Education before university\n7. Bad teaching red flags\n8. External source notes\n9. Study advice for surviving the current system","color":"5"},{"id":"1d6ec6e684f6aed1","type":"text","x":-1650,"y":560,"width":1350,"height":230,"text":"## Clarified context\n\n- “Degrees completely mis-chosen” means that students often choose the wrong degree for themselves.\n- Degree programs themselves can also be badly designed, but that is a separate point.\n- The small pilot is only one possible idea. This canvas is a collection of research, problems, and ideas, not an active pilot.\n\nOne original note is still unfinished: the grades note ends with “depends on the subject, the teacher, and...”.","color":"3"},{"id":"6a282353f4d6f81e","type":"text","x":-1600,"y":2520,"width":1300,"height":90,"text":"# Assessment and grades","color":"1"},{"id":"4452f0411febc1c8","type":"text","x":-200,"y":2520,"width":1300,"height":90,"text":"# Learning culture and motivation","color":"2"},{"id":"cab7cbd8a199ca1b","type":"text","x":1200,"y":2520,"width":1300,"height":90,"text":"# Projects","color":"5"},{"id":"1413bfb5fdc0520a","type":"text","x":2600,"y":2520,"width":1300,"height":90,"text":"# Teaching, relevance, and degree problems","color":"6"},{"id":"6d56844a3b0940b0","type":"text","x":5600,"y":100,"width":1100,"height":310,"text":"## Working principles\n\nThis is an organizational synthesis added from the original notes.\n\n- Learning should matter more than grading.\n- Evaluation should be continuous, broad, and based on real understanding.\n- Students should solve problems, build things, search for information, and explain their reasoning.\n- Projects should include feedback during construction and transparent contributions.\n- Students should have more choice and progress at a suitable pace.\n- Teachers should guide and teach, not act as unquestionable masters.\n- Cooperation and everyday peer learning should be normal.\n- AI can make individual, patient teaching available at scale.","color":"5"},{"id":"4d10a5a670cbf6cb","type":"text","x":4400,"y":2550,"width":2300,"height":145,"text":"# Research directions and possible experiments\n\nThis section contains independent research ideas, reference models, questions, and possible things to test. It is not a committed pilot or implementation plan.","color":"4"},{"id":"f6b31bb680b5d267","type":"text","x":4400,"y":3780,"width":2300,"height":360,"text":"## Questions to explore\n\nThese are optional research questions, not a current implementation plan.\n\n- Which problems are most important, and which are downstream symptoms?\n- What should higher education be for?\n- Which university functions should stay together, and which should be separated?\n- How could understanding be represented without reducing each person to one number?\n- How could peer review account for bias, friendships, differences in knowledge, and relevance?\n- How could individual contributions to group projects be made visible?\n- How should teachers be selected, evaluated, and supported?\n- How should a system support students who still do not know what they want?\n- Which ideas, if any, would be worth testing through small experiments?","color":"3"},{"id":"ec1acd2f631bf136","type":"text","x":-1600,"y":5700,"width":3600,"height":220,"text":"# External source notes\n\nThese nodes summarize videos, articles, and comments collected during research. They are source notes, not necessarily my own views. I kept strong or controversial claims in their original framing instead of silently adopting, correcting, or weakening them.","color":"6"},{"id":"9e52c7e780b711c4","type":"text","x":-1500,"y":6150,"width":2000,"height":110,"text":"## Competition, research, vocation, and lifelong learning","color":"6"},{"id":"fdbc92b6d2f67fc0","type":"text","x":800,"y":6150,"width":1600,"height":110,"text":"## Stress, passing, and the loss of curiosity","color":"6"},{"id":"4e3ddea59cb58537","type":"text","x":2700,"y":6150,"width":1900,"height":110,"text":"## School rules, freedom, sleep, and project learning","color":"6"},{"id":"f532b52cefa34642","type":"text","x":4900,"y":6150,"width":2400,"height":110,"text":"## Purpose, problem-first learning, grades, and alternatives to college","color":"6"},{"id":"642aee981335824b","type":"text","x":7600,"y":6150,"width":2000,"height":110,"text":"## Classical education and criticism of modern universities","color":"6"},{"id":"9762e05eb3018a6f","type":"text","x":9900,"y":6150,"width":700,"height":110,"text":"## Surveillance in education","color":"6"},{"id":"58a962d70154ab50","type":"text","x":-1500,"y":8700,"width":4000,"height":100,"text":"## Additional reading","color":"6"},{"id":"7580733a671067f2","type":"text","x":-3500,"y":120,"width":600,"height":100,"text":"## Hacker News discussion notes","color":"6"},{"id":"95bce8eb57f29412","type":"text","x":-2800,"y":120,"width":700,"height":100,"text":"## Karpathy’s study advice notes","color":"6"},{"id":"40208c834c2bd7ab","type":"text","text":"## “Checkbox” tests\n\n**Checkbox** *(multiple choice, single right choice)* tests are the most common way to evaluate my expertise on a topic they teach.\n\n- They use too small a sample size.\n- They are hard to write well. Most teachers do not know how to make good checkbox tests.\n\t- Most make it too obvious. Even without knowing much about the topic, you can eliminate a couple of options. Teachers often create the incorrect answers around the correct answer and make them unbelievable.\n- They do not test what I actually know, but what I do not know. This *might* be the wrong way to test someone’s knowledge.\n\t- They do not even measure what you know, but what you get right.\n- Evaluating four months of work in two hours through 20 questions where you choose an answer is unrealistic.\n- I can simply be having a very good day, or a *bad* day.","x":-1600,"y":2670,"width":1300,"height":310},{"id":"f2d74526e5c2ed3f","type":"text","text":"## Projects need feedback during construction\n\nProjects are good, but a single evaluation point is also a huge miss. They should be evaluated throughout their construction, not just through the final presentation. A student can take a wrong turn at the start, forget to ask the teacher before continuing, and face very bad consequences later.\n\nIf students were evaluated **while** building the project, or at least through some kind of *sprints*, they could redirect it as professors give feedback. The end result would be much better, and the student would **actually** learn while building instead of being graded only on the final result.","x":1200,"y":2670,"width":1300,"height":200},{"id":"6237f28886640a26","type":"text","text":"## Exams encourage grading, not learning\n\nAfter, for example, failing an exam, most students do not care that they do not know jack sh\\*t about the topic. They only care that they failed. They do not try to learn from their mistakes.\n\nThis is even worse for students who did not fail. If you get just above the grade you wanted or expected, you will probably never check the exam correction, if it is even provided. In my case, it was rarely provided in a useful way. Exam revision was usually possible, but it was too inconvenient for even me to attend. I had to go to the university at a specific time just to **check** the exam. I could not even take useful notes from it. These reviews mostly exist so that you can check whether the teacher made a grading mistake, not so that you can learn from what you missed.\n\nStudents then completely forget everything within days or weeks. This is from personal experience, once again, ymmv.\n\nIt seems that tests, and the current education system as a whole, are **simply** about grading. They are not about actually learning and leaving with more knowledge that you *can apply in real life* than you had when you entered.","x":-1600,"y":3040,"width":1300,"height":340},{"id":"6c401365341b90ad","type":"text","text":"## Everyday interaction and peer learning\n\nMore emphasis should be given to simply talking, interacting, and networking. I do not mean more company networking events. Those already exist, and there are many of them. I mean that it should be normal for students to talk to each other and to the teacher, and to learn from other people personally every day.\n\nIt is not the norm, though. It seems like students only “want” (and want is a big word in this case) in the classroom, during the time they are assigned to do so.","x":-200,"y":3518,"width":1300,"height":205},{"id":"2557aee20da9a019","type":"text","text":"## Studying for exams instead of learning\n\nPeople just study for exams. It should not be like this. As you learn something in school, you should start knowing it and becoming curious about it.\n\nMost students do not do any of this because they could not care less.","x":-200,"y":3302,"width":1300,"height":156,"color":"2"},{"id":"b04d9431185f455b","type":"text","text":"## Many students do not want to be there\n\nIt feels like many people either feel forced, have no other solution, or simply feel the need to study something that they do not care about or want to study.","x":-200,"y":3124,"width":1300,"height":118,"color":"3"},{"id":"6d5efdb323892f0d","type":"text","text":"### What's wrong with the current higher education system?\n> Disclaimer: all this is coming from a Computer Science & Engineering student @ [FEUP](https://fe.up.pt/) *(Portugal)*, ymmv","x":-1600,"y":2300,"width":2700,"height":170,"color":"1"},{"id":"206595ff6f08b7f0","type":"text","text":"## Grades dominate the incentives\n\nThe whole system is just numbers. Grades are basically everything they make you strive for.\n\nThere **are** projects and incentives you can enter, but they are not given nearly the same level of importance.","x":-200,"y":2670,"width":1300,"height":156},{"id":"87c5f0e2b2765e28","type":"text","text":"## Boring teaching\n\nSubjects are taught in a boring manner.","x":2600,"y":2670,"width":1300,"height":118,"color":"6"},{"id":"707c1a2bc3da8395","type":"text","text":"## Students often choose the wrong degree\n\nStudents often choose a degree that is wrong for them.","x":2600,"y":2848,"width":1300,"height":125,"color":"6"},{"id":"c9b408acb26e2f74","type":"text","text":"## Time-limited exams\n\nSome exams should allow more time. Not all exams, but some become so time-bound that it makes no sense. This depends on the material, of course, but in many cases the exam only just feels possible within the time the teacher set.","x":-1600,"y":3440,"width":1300,"height":140},{"id":"d0371e28c3de8ce8","type":"text","text":"## “Factory-monkey” projects\n\n“Factory-monkey” type projects *(this feels very specific to my course, but we had a Programming group project where everything we had to do was literally laid out in front of us)* should not exist. They create factory workers who only do what they are told, because that is all they learn how to do.\n\nThis type of project should not exist as-is. Once again, creativity and critical thinking should be emphasized more.","x":1200,"y":3152,"width":1300,"height":178},{"id":"996851582ccb4b82","type":"text","text":"## Group project contributions are opaque\n\nWhile building group projects, you do not actually know how much each student does. Group activities and projects are important, but they should be much more transparent. This could use GitHub commit history, some kind of event log, more open conversations, or something similar. I do not really know how this would be implemented. It also would not matter as much if the whole grading system were not so number-focused.","x":1200,"y":2930,"width":1300,"height":162},{"id":"1cd66df557247172","type":"text","text":"## People become numbers\n\nPeople basically become numbers. They should not. Each person’s abilities are completely unique. This feels *cliché* to say, but it is true. Pretending that one number can definitively say how much someone knows about a topic, whether broad or narrow, is a fallacy.\n\nPeople should be described through qualities. **What** they know about something, how they apply it, how they learned it, and so on.","x":-200,"y":2886,"width":1300,"height":178},{"id":"bcd970b900946a35","type":"text","text":"## Problem solving and finding information\n\nProblem solving should be given more emphasis, as should discovering solutions to problems.\n\nWe have the internet, and now LLMs. Nobody needs to recall a formula from memory in 0.1 seconds. Finding and searching for information is really, really important.","x":4400,"y":1660,"width":1100,"height":178},{"id":"7b6b27050b31f4b7","type":"text","text":"## More choice, less mandatory work\n\nThere should be less mandatory work and more optional work that you can **choose** to do.","x":5600,"y":1790,"width":1100,"height":118},{"id":"11cceaf379455d0e","type":"text","text":"## Education in the age of AI\n\nIt feels cringe to say, but it is true. Knowledge workers have definitely changed forever.\n\nOne-to-one learning has changed too. It is no longer unfathomable for every student to have an infinitely patient teacher that knows every subject really, really well and can teach each student at their own pace.","x":5600,"y":760,"width":1100,"height":178},{"id":"6e55420f45d07374","type":"text","text":"- Reads directly off slides or PDFs without explaining\n- Uses someone else's slides\n\t- It's not bad if they're genuinely good slideshows, but most of the time they are just using them out of laziness and the slides are often outdated or even plain wrong\n- Tells students to \"just read it yourselves\"\n- Teaches out of order, jumps between chapters, or mixes topics incoherently (without a good reason to do so)\n- Goes on long, **irrelevant tangents**\n- Provides no examples or meaningful explanations\n- Assigns work unrelated (or marginally related) to what was taught\n- Makes exams waaay harder than most/all homework and assignments they gave out\n- Refuses to answer questions or says \"watch the video again\"\n- Uses cold calling aggressively or mockingly\n- Uses anti-scientific or factually wrong material *(it happens!)*\n- Forces note-taking at an unreasonable pace","x":9000,"y":2320,"width":1500,"height":330},{"id":"3bf79dbd6331bbb9","type":"text","text":"## Poor teaching techniques","x":9000,"y":2180,"width":1500,"height":90,"color":"3"},{"id":"1927eaf2ab638174","type":"text","text":"## Attitude and mindset issues","x":7300,"y":2180,"width":1500,"height":90,"color":"5"},{"id":"a380775b697b6c29","type":"text","text":"- Brags that most students fail or that the class is \"hard to pass\"; treats high failure rates as a flex/badge of honor\n- Says things like \"that’s trivial\" or mocks students (even publicly) for not understanding; treats beginners like experts and lacks empathy; makes passive-aggressive or openly aggressive remarks\n- Complains about teaching, money, or not wanting to be there\n- Acts arrogant, superior, or dismissive of questions; blames students for basically everything","x":7300,"y":2320,"width":1500,"height":132},{"id":"8eac15b120e5b65b","type":"text","text":"- Does not respond to emails (or is hostile when replying)\n- Gives vague, dismissive, or angry replies\n- Refuses to clarify assignments or policies\n- Doesn't accept feedback or *claims they never receive any*","x":9000,"y":3400,"width":1500,"height":132},{"id":"091346db70ff52cb","type":"text","text":"- Exams don’t match lectures, homework, or reviews\n- Grades arbitrarily or inconsistently\n- Loses assignments or marks submitted work as missing\n- Penalizes students for trivial formatting issues\n- Threatens expulsion over minor technicalities\n- Uses peer grading unfairly as a major grade component\n- Changes grading rules mid-semester\n- Gives different rules to different students","x":7300,"y":4170,"width":1500,"height":220},{"id":"d2b672efd12aec05","type":"text","text":"## Assessment issues","x":7300,"y":4030,"width":1500,"height":90,"color":"6"},{"id":"05528dd7812a1f58","type":"text","text":"- No syllabus, incomplete syllabus, or changes it constantly\n- Syllabus contradicts LMS (Canvas, Moodle, etc.)\n- LMS is disorganized or clearly neglected\n- Doesn't post grades or returns work **only at the end of the semester**\n- Says \"it's your job to track your grade, not mine\"\n- Forgets what was taught or confuses different classes, often\n- Mishandles \"extra credit\"\n- Gives unclear or missing instructions","x":7300,"y":3400,"width":1500,"height":220},{"id":"dc5aa724b10d2949","type":"text","text":"## Communication issues","x":9000,"y":3260,"width":1500,"height":90,"color":"2"},{"id":"860439a9b2c07f06","type":"text","text":"## Organization issues","x":7300,"y":3260,"width":1500,"height":90,"color":"5"},{"id":"0e526d710cb0f989","type":"text","text":"## Professionalism issues","x":9000,"y":4030,"width":1500,"height":90,"color":"4"},{"id":"2f9ebfeb50ea7214","type":"text","text":"- Publicly shames classes for being “the worst”\n- Talks excessively about their own career instead of teaching\n- Bullies students or talks about them behind their backs\n- Makes sexist or biased remarks\n- Uses fear, intimidation, or humiliation as \"motivation\"\n- Refuses accommodations or accessibility options\n- Encourages students to drop the course instead of helping them succeed","x":9000,"y":4170,"width":1500,"height":198},{"id":"be6c1b210a74e5a0","type":"text","text":"## Possible idea: a small pilot\n\nOne possible idea is to run a tiny pilot, even with 30 students, prove outcomes first, and only then scale. It could involve students who finish high school, genuinely want to learn, and want to take a gap year.\n\nThis is only one idea in the collection. It is not a current pilot.","x":4400,"y":2720,"width":1100,"height":225},{"id":"595f1943a3c3f8d9","type":"text","text":"## Model to study\n\nLook at Duale Ausbildung.","x":5600,"y":2720,"width":1100,"height":118},{"id":"9f1b6a7e87d21e57","type":"text","text":"## Anonymous decision questionnaire\n\nCreate an anonymous questionnaire. Ask participants to be abundantly honest, with nothing to hide, and to reflect their true thoughts at the time they made their decisions.\n\n- Why did you come to university?\n- Why did you choose _this_ university?\n- Why did you choose this degree or program?\n- Did you consider working or trying other things before coming to university? Why or why not?\n- Do you think it was the right decision? If you could go back, would you do the same?","x":4400,"y":3100,"width":1100,"height":266},{"id":"f772988de899c2ba","type":"text","text":"## Good ideas and initiatives\n\n- [42 School](https://www.42network.org/about-us/mission/)\n- [Open Source Society University](https://ossu.dev/)\n- [Math Academy](https://www.mathacademy.com/)\n- [Astra Nova](https://www.astranova.org/)\n- [3b1b](https://www.youtube.com/@3blue1brown)\n- [Khan Academy](https://www.khanacademy.org/)\n- [Alpha School](https://alpha.school/)","x":5600,"y":2940,"width":1100,"height":250},{"id":"eb8a2d105577f479","type":"text","text":"## Bad teachers are too common\n\nIn my experience, bad teachers are especially common in private institutions, but they also exist in public ones.\n\n> [!warning]\n> Clear signs of bad teachers or bad teaching:","x":7950,"y":1750,"width":800,"height":204},{"id":"d3882d06cefb8d2c","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/q7MXwhIGJvA","x":7300,"y":1750,"width":600,"height":340},{"id":"05bd6072ec6f246c","type":"text","text":"# Education before university\n\nUp until high school, everything is mostly fine.\n\nThere are no major flaws. I think children do need _quite a bit_ of structure, and they need to learn how to behave. Most of this should be the responsibility of parents, not school teachers, but still.\n\nI would structure it like this:\n\n- **1st to 4th grade**, the basics: ages 6 to 9\n- **5th to 8th grade**, the essentials: ages 10 to 13\n- **9th to 11th grade**: ages 14 to 17\n\nThat said, I will not focus much on education before high school. It could be better, sure, but it is not terrible. I do think a strong amount of structure and fundamentals is necessary at this stage. These are the foundations of knowledge: mathematics, some science and physics, English, physical education, philosophy, logic or programming, and something creative such as drawing, cooking, music, writing, theater, or using computer programs like GIMP.\n\nFor physical education, students could choose a sport at the beginning of the period and stick with it because they enjoy it. Everyone in the school would practice physical education at the same time. It would include some basic running and strength or endurance training, followed by a selection of sports each student can choose from. Schools should also teach more basic nutrition.","x":7300,"y":100,"width":1400,"height":466},{"id":"dc9c35e7bb73bb62","type":"text","text":"# What university currently does\n\nUniversity is currently a bundle or bubble. It does many different jobs. Here are the main issues with each one.","x":2000,"y":100,"width":1900,"height":170,"color":"5"},{"id":"9e43197e4aea4fdd","type":"text","text":"**Research** is about discovering things that no one knew before.\n**Education** is about discovering things you didn't know before!\n\nUniversities are the only one that do/offer both.\n\nProblems:\n1. They are ==trying to solve their problems through **Competition**==.\n\t- They compete for students\n\t- They compete for the best teachers and researchers\n\t- They compete to make speedy discoveries and publishing them\n\t\t- To compete for more money to do more research to have more students [...]\n\t- This competition-based approach has to be replaced with a cooperation-based approach.\n2. The focus of ==higher education should not be a preparation for a vocation==.\n\t- We've just got no idea what jobs will exist in the future!\n\t\t- Creativity, adaptability, ability to change;\n\t- Believes that universities shouldn't be dominated by teaching vocational skills (professional/specialized abilities for hands-on application), but instead focus on development of critical thinking, writing, and productive cooperation skills.\n\t\t- Most vocational skills can just be learned on the job\n\t- Also thinks it's possible to have a higher education system (post secondary school) for lifelong learning, interwoven with work (and universities become places for shorter visits - learn as you go).\n3. Work on sustainability needs more. Students expect more, our societies need more.\n\n> [!quote]\n> ==Micro courses on specific topics== or aimed at specific skills will be a normal part of working life and universities must adapt to make these offerings as well or they risk being made irrelevant as they're left with a dwindling market for full-time students","x":-500,"y":6320,"width":1000,"height":601},{"id":"64b2b19fcb76139c","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/W1QZwzGlwlQ","x":-1500,"y":6320,"width":900,"height":500},{"id":"6eda5f9dcb7017fa","type":"text","text":"Believes the school system is designed to cause the students stress, anxiety, and it ==kills the passion of learning==.\n\nShould be more about letting students develop an intelectual mindset and a passion for learning.\n\nShould encourage more students to conduct their own research on topics that strike their interest and curiosity; children might actually enjoy reading!\n\nSchool, as of now, is not really about learning; it's about *passing*.\n\nHe mastered the art of *short-term memorization* (and that's what's important for school!): cramming for tests.\n\nBelieves schools are designed to send students to college.\n\n> [!poem]\n> I always hated school\n> And I’m not saying that\n> Just to look cool\n> For 13 years of my life, I sat\n> \n> In those chairs\n> And played along\n> Acutely aware\n> That I didn’t belong\n> \n> In the broken system\n> That kills the joy for learning\n> Intelligence vs wisdom\n> A young mind yearning\n> \n> To create\n> Without the restrictions\n> A constant state\n> Of contradiction\n> \n> Standardized testing\n> To prove that you’re smart\n> No time for resting\n> Forget about art\n> \n> Pointless rules\n> And obligations\n> Lack of tools\n> For compensation\n> \n> Producing productive members of\n> Society\n> This isn’t a one-size-fits-all glove;\n> Variety\n> \n> Is what they need\n> Show them their options\n> Then let them succeed\n> Free from the toxins\n> \n> Of our broken education system","x":1550,"y":6320,"width":800,"height":1263},{"id":"005f24422aaddb04","type":"text","text":"- ==Teachers do not explain why kids are learning a subject==\n\t- You just sort of get dumped into math and wonder\n\t\t- \"Why am I learning this?\"\n\t\t- \"What is the point of this?\"\n\t\t- \"I don't know why I am being asked to do these strange problems\"\n\t- The **why** of things is extremely important\n\t- Our brain has evolved to discard things it deems irrelevant!\n\t\t- \"It seems irrelevant, but I am being told to remember these random formulas and I'll be punished if I don't remember them, so I better remember them\"\n- ==Teach to a problem, not the tools!==\n\t- Picking a problem and then using various educational tools to solve that problem (say math, physics, economics) to solve that problem is far more engaging than just teaching the tools\n\t\t- \"We are going to take apart this engine, see how it works and put it back together again\"\n\t\t- In that process, we learn about wrenches and screwdrivers etc. etc. and in the course of solving the problem of taking the engine apart and putting it back together, you learn about all the tools that you need and now you definitely understand the relevance!\n- Thinks there is no need to even have a college degree\n\t- It may be an indicator, but it's not necessarily the case\n\t\t- Think Bill Gates, Larry Ellison, Steve Jobs, ...\n\t- In this day and age, everything is available basically for free\n\t\t- There is, however, a value that college has which is\n\t\t\t- Seeing if somebody can work hard at something (including a bunch of annoying homework assignments), and still do everything regardless of how they feel *(soldier through)*\n\t\t\t- Also allows you to hang around a bunch of people your age instead of going right into *workforce*\n\t\t\t- So colleges are basically ==for fun== and to ==prove you can do your chores==, but they're not for learning\n\t\t\t- Tesla has a requirement to experience you can display. Not a degree.\n- It should not be this huge chore\n\t- The more you can gamify the process of \"downloading data and algorithms\" into your brain, the better\n\t\t- Kids can play videogames on autopilot all day. Make the experience of learning more interactive and engaging.\n\t\t- Disconnect the \"level/grade\" thing from the subjects\n\t\t\t- ==Allow each person to progress at the fastest pace that they can or are interested in, for each subject==\n\t- Usually, \"You got someone standing up there kind of lecturing on people, and they've done the same lecture 20 years in a row, and they're not very excited about it and that lack of enthusiasm is conveyed to the students - they're not very excited about it. They don't know why they're there\".\n\t\t- \"Why are we teaching people these things?\" - kids **need purpose**\n- Elon Musk created his own school \"Ad Astra\" *(to the stars)* (now spun up [Astra Nova](https://www.astranova.org))\n\t- There aren't any grades (all children go at the same time)\n\t\t- Each child has a different rate of learning - tailor the learning to each kid's aptitudes and abilities \n\t- Of course teaching to a problem, not tools\n\t- His kids *love* going to school\n\t- He tries to teach & think ==*from first principles*==\n\t\t- \"Let's look at physics and economics of a rocket\" and say \"If we analyze the physics of the rocket, is it physically possible?\" Yes.\n\t\t- Don't be bound by what has already been done!\n\t\t\t- Go back to the drawing board!\n\t- Focus on critical thinking\n- Most things in school are opposite of what it takes to be successful in real life (Kim Kiyosaki)\n\t- \"Don't make mistakes\"\n\t- \"Do as you're told\"\n\t- \"Take tests by yourself/on your own; don't cooperate\"\n\t- \"Do it by yourself\"\n\t- \"There is only one right answer\"\n\t- Everything opposite to what it should be\n- Schools should teach more about money","x":4900,"y":7120,"width":2400,"height":1056},{"id":"43c8896d02f29981","type":"text","text":"- Teachers\n\t- Hit or miss\n\t- More money to teachers (?)\n- Sleep\n\t- Thinks 7h30 is too early\n\t- Should start at 9am\n\t- Re-introducing nap time for ~30 min\n- Drawing should not be punished\n- Rules\n\t- The 4 levels of freedom/tiers of learning\n\t\t- 1) No phone; always being watched; obligatory tutoring provided by the school (with the extra funding; other students can also attend the tutoring though!)\n\t\t- 2) Basically how school is right now\n\t\t- 3) (the default) Listen to music all the time; use computers however you like; don't have any *mandatory* homework\n\t\t- 4) You can leave you classes whenever you want; show up to other classes whenever you want; basically  doing anything that's not disruptive; still have to be at school 3/4ths of the time\n\t- You have to wait a whole semester to get back into tier 4 if you got kicked out\n\t- The student grades are just A, A-, B, B-, C...\n\t\t- And their average generally correspond with the 4 tiers of learning (A/A-: 4; B/B-: 3; C/C-: 2; ...: 1), but can change depending on behaviour\n- If the teacher is done with the material for the day, you're free to go!\n- He also renamed the year grades but that's not really relevant here\n\nAlso from a comment:\n> How I would change this is as followed\n> 1. Project based learning to give purpose to assignments rather than random problems\n> 2. Homework isn’t always necessary and doesn’t always change your grade, as it’s a tool for learning so only homework assignments that relate to the project(like a script or something) should be mandatory\n> 3. Late work within 3 days time will still give full credit, unless you ask the teacher for an extension or is otherwise stated to fix the issue with grades\n> 4. Classes should teach more hands on lessons to account for tactile learners\n> 5. Tests are now once a week on fridays with significantly less difficulty and are used to show that you have been learning throughout the week, they are also used as a sort of benchmark in order to determine whether the class needs more learning time or if they should move on\n\n> [!disclaimer]\n> This is for school up until the end of high school, not university. Though there are still some valid-ish ideas we can extract from here","x":3550,"y":6460,"width":1000,"height":872},{"id":"5e47b2d1b4ddd288","type":"text","text":"Straight from the video","x":3550,"y":6320,"width":1000,"height":90,"color":"#ff9494"},{"id":"95fa05c1ef37ed6d","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/i3w3_w9G1n0","x":800,"y":6320,"width":700,"height":400},{"id":"f06a17c610a91a55","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/FCu_Yu9EwOw","x":4900,"y":6720,"width":700,"height":350},{"id":"36e3f5b83a229b92","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/IG2zSQ27VFQ","x":5700,"y":6720,"width":700,"height":350},{"id":"9eb697401f112b16","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/2IyJIZHgRvw","x":5700,"y":6320,"width":700,"height":350},{"id":"347689399f21302a","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/YoTc8LgY-RA","x":6500,"y":6320,"width":700,"height":350},{"id":"4c6801863bd002c9","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/YNQDp3v-VGE","x":6500,"y":6720,"width":700,"height":350},{"id":"fbf101ecb3140d67","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/tDGWLXf2vRE","x":2700,"y":6320,"width":800,"height":440},{"id":"ffecdb8cf8377855","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/O-QbVkyi8yc","x":4900,"y":6320,"width":700,"height":350},{"id":"0a17e9372dff2e35","type":"text","text":"## Assessment, discovery, and class format\n\nA better education system would include peer review of work, while accounting for bias, friendships, differences in knowledge, and relevance. It would involve reviewing assignments, covering broad topics, and using random daily questions to check whether the student truly _understands_ the subject.\n\nThe system must be standardized. Otherwise, evaluators will simply give everyone top grades. Reviews should be anonymous, and the most intelligent people should not be pushed out of universities.\n\nAt the end of the day, achieving a truly effective education system also requires changing how jobs work.\n\nIn physics, for example, before learning the value of _g_, or even that it exists, students should first frame the problem of falling, run experiments, and eventually arrive at the value of _g_ themselves. Only then should we discuss how others historically approached the problem, how it was framed, and how gravity was named and formalized. This would be “the ideal world.”\n\nClasses would not have fixed durations. Instead, they would continue until the audience, meaning the students, votes to end them.","x":4400,"y":760,"width":1100,"height":378,"color":"5"},{"id":"0892404cb36611fa","type":"text","text":"## Proposed solution\n> In the “ideal world”\n\nFor skeptics who believe there cannot be a better system and claim that we are at the peak of knowledge: **never before has there been as much knowledge as there is today**.\n\nLess than 1,000 years ago, it was normal to be a generalist. There were very few specialists because there was nowhere near the amount of knowledge about humanity, the natural world, or science that exists now.\n\nThe current education system was developed “in a rush” to handle this volume of knowledge and to produce specialists. Of course, it is not perfect, and of course there are better ways to deal with this.","x":4400,"y":100,"width":1100,"height":281,"color":"2"},{"id":"52ffb2ebf9e212e2","type":"text","text":"## Teachers as guides, not masters\n\nThere should not be as much of a “master” role for teachers as there is now. There should simply be people who know more than you and who can and **want** to teach you. Teachers think they have everything figured out when they do not.","x":5600,"y":1190,"width":1100,"height":140},{"id":"80e7e717b80302d7","type":"text","text":"## Incentives to improve\n\nThere should be a much clearer and stronger incentive for everyone to become smarter and get better at what they are studying.","x":5600,"y":1490,"width":1100,"height":118},{"id":"2318e41e247138bf","type":"link","url":"https://hilariusbookbinder.substack.com/p/the-average-college-student-today","x":-1500,"y":8860,"width":1900,"height":900},{"id":"aebdeb7997367e7f","type":"link","url":"https://medium.com/my-pewsonal-bwog/standardized-tests-too-crude-to-be-used-7afb0715a3e2","x":550,"y":8860,"width":1900,"height":900},{"id":"ac9816c70d0b5369","type":"text","text":"## Learning\n\nThis is what people think is the sole purpose of university, but it actually is not. It is the main function I want to tackle with the new system.","x":2000,"y":330,"width":900,"height":140},{"id":"e40b0f5ed7229998","type":"text","text":"## Employment and infrastructure\n\nUniversity also exists as a job for teachers and all the other staff needed to keep the facilities and infrastructure running.","x":3000,"y":330,"width":900,"height":140},{"id":"fd949e63862715b6","type":"text","text":"## Leaving home\n\nSimply for leaving the house, or getting away from parents, etc.","x":3000,"y":1230,"width":900,"height":118},{"id":"e9a750c69b8663e5","type":"text","text":"## Maturation and direction\n\nBabysitting and maturation for young adults who still do not know what to do with their lives.","x":3000,"y":580,"width":900,"height":118},{"id":"c3e69b85cc590897","type":"text","text":"## Research and knowledge production\n\n- Drives university prestige and rankings.\n- Staff are often hired and promoted for research output, not teaching ability.\n- Knowledge is often locked behind paywalls or developed as R&D for external entities.\n- Often creates a direct conflict with the **Learning** bubble.","x":2000,"y":1230,"width":900,"height":184},{"id":"97512e905ca4f89a","type":"text","text":"## An insurance policy\n\nAn insurance policy for parents and for the student.\n\n- “Just go to university or college.”\n- “I do not know what I am going to do, but I will just go to university.”","x":3000,"y":900,"width":900,"height":178},{"id":"8f8689ee64149323","type":"text","text":"## Socializing, parties, events, and networking\n\n- People just get drunk.\n- Sometimes you cannot really choose which people you want to spend time with, learn from, or work with in group projects.","x":2000,"y":900,"width":900,"height":162},{"id":"72c7b2f82975e03c","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/MDzS4ed6tgk","x":7600,"y":6320,"width":1000,"height":560},{"id":"a7bb225664376221","type":"text","text":"- What education used to be (for 2,500 years):\n\t- Cultivating your soul/spirit\n\t- Teaching virtues and how to live a good life\n\t- Understanding how knowledge connects together\n\t- Humanities at the center (philosophy, history, languages, literature, art)\n- Now nobody agrees what it's for\n\t- Most people think: school -> grades -> job\n1. Mass education ruined everything\n\t- Started 1800s to create obedient workers for factories\n\t- It's fundamentally job training, not soul cultivation\n\t- Had to water down content to teach 100x more people\n\t    - An 1913 8th grade test stumps Ivy League students today\n\t- Everyone \"needs\" college now, but degrees are worth less than ever\n\t- Lots of debt for degrees people don't actually need for their jobs\n2. Hyper-specialization killed holistic learning\n\t- Mid-1800s: explosion of knowledge (especially science)\n\t- Response: everyone became narrow experts\n\t- \"Jack of all trades\" used to be a compliment - now it's an insult\n\t- Humanities got an inferiority complex and copied the sciences\n\t    - Started hyper-specializing (makes no sense for literature/history)\n\t- Professors write incomprehensible jargon about incredibly narrow topics\n\t- Nobody talks across disciplines\n\t- Lots of meaningless work nobody can call out\n3. Marxist oppressor-oppressed ideology took over\n\t- Been spreading for 50-70 years, exploded in 1980s-90s\n\t- Professors introducing themselves as \"I'm a Marxist feminist\"\n\t- Everything viewed through: race, class, gender, oppressor vs oppressed\n\t- Not seeking knowledge - they have an axe to grind\n\t- \"Dead white males\" of classical education are the enemy\n\t- Can say you're a Marxist but not a fascist (should sound equally bad)\n4. Bureaucratic bloat\n\t- Past 30 years: explosion of administrators (not teachers)\n\t- Administrators make more than professors\n\t- Tuition inflation way higher than general inflation\n\t    - Example: $10,000/year → $100,000/year\n5. DEI ideology and culture wars\n\t- Many universities require DEI statements\n\t- Atmosphere is \"Kafkaesque\" or \"Orwellian\"\n\t- Cancel culture, safe spaces, loss of free speech\n\t    - Can't say what you think or you'll be canceled\n\t- \"Each time you stay silent, it eats away at your soul a bit more\"\n\t- Exact opposite of teaching virtue and strength\n\t- Truly toxic environment\n6. Too many non-academic programs\n\t- Colleges teaching \"hotel front desk management\" and other hands-on job skills\n\t- These don't belong in academic institutions\n\t- Contributes to the bloat and watering down\n- Solutions\n\t- Short term:\n\t\t- Hold your nose and get through it if you need a degree\n\t\t- Find best institution that still holds old ideals\n\t\t- Only real advantage: access to great libraries\n\t- Let it implode:\n\t\t- Too many schools, not enough students\n\t\t- Many institutions will fail (especially newer ones)\n\t\t- This needs to happen to clear the garbage\n\t- Alumni pressure:\n\t\t- Write letters to your alma mater\n\t\t- \"You educated me with these values, now you're abandoning them\"\n\t- Best solution: Private academies (new model)\n\t\t- Back to Plato/Aristotle model - study with a master teacher\n\t\t- Online learning makes this feasible\n\t\t- Focus on:\n\t\t    - Great books\n\t\t    - Classical education\n\t\t    - Languages (Latin, Greek)\n\t\t    - No bureaucracy, no DEI, no culture wars\n\t\t- Key innovation: **lifelong learning**\n\t\t    - Not \"cram everything by age 22\"\n\t\t    - 1-2 hours/week reading hard books throughout your life\n\t\t    - Decades-long projects\n\t\t- These become \"keepers of the flame\" as universities implode","x":7600,"y":6940,"width":2000,"height":1628},{"id":"7f7b3d2388d04292","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/5qpXypoVx1M","x":9900,"y":6320,"width":700,"height":350},{"id":"02e87a4914c19f5b","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/EqVO7NF9VOE","x":9900,"y":6720,"width":700,"height":350},{"id":"c2fabea0103da462","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/dqTTojTija8","x":9900,"y":7120,"width":700,"height":300},{"id":"2ae928def8c5fd1d","type":"text","text":"Conditioning young people to be OK with being watched 24/7","x":9900,"y":7480,"width":700,"height":80},{"id":"6a114ffee589e9ff","type":"text","text":"# My personal study advice\n\nFor the *current* education system (tailored for studying for getting great grades at University).\n\n## What is studying?\n\n- Studying isn't anything special; it's just learning material to answer questions on a test.\n- There's no need to fear subjects; they're just a collection of simple things.\n- Being engaged with the material is important - follow classes and pay attention day-to-day.\n    - Watch related videos in your free time (e.g., on the subway).\n\n## What people do wrong\n\n- Studying for too long in one session (> 2-3 hours).\n- Studying too much in one day (> 5 hours).\n- Multitasking - it doesn't work!\n- Not getting enough sleep - sleep is crucial for learning.\n- Saying no to commitments because of exams.\n    - The day has 16 hours; you won't study all 16 hours.\n    - 3 extra hours won't significantly change your grade.\n\n## How I study (and it works!)\n\n- Start 3-5 days before at most.\n- **ALWAYS** do past and mock exams.\n    - Prioritize over worksheets.\n- Solve everything quickly.\n    - If you can't solve it and you know you can't, look at the solution and learn.\n    - If you already know it, move on.\n- Don't make summaries (unless I really need to memorize formulas).\n    - Quickly check others' summaries (10 min) just to confirm.\n- Always have a friend who understands more.\n    - And be that friend for others (explaining helps you learn and clear out doubts you didn't even know you had/stuff you thought you knew deeply but actually only memorized).\n\n## During the exam (tricks I use)\n\n- Leave the hardest questions for last.\n\t- Sometimes that means leaving 50% of the questions for last; that's fine.\n- Do everything quickly first, then review.\n- Eat chocolate/some sort of sugary food beforehand (personal ritual).\n- Never leave anything blank.\n    - Think like the teacher.\n    - For multiple-choice, eliminate options and pick the most likely.\n\n## Other techniques I recommend (but don’t use)\n\n- Active recall - force your brain to remember.\n- Study a little, often.\n- Learn with multiple senses (see, hear, talk about the material) *(I do sometimes)*","x":-4200,"y":240,"width":650,"height":1296,"color":"4"},{"id":"cba99104a7a73702","type":"text","text":"## Filtering and sorting in society\n\nWhen hiring someone, university sometimes matters. Employers ask: “Did they go to university?” “Where did they go?” “Did they finish?” “What grade did they finish with?” They trust the filtering that the institution already performed.","x":2000,"y":580,"width":900,"height":162},{"id":"5c9688bb58730256","type":"link","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=45635533","x":-3500,"y":270,"width":600,"height":330},{"id":"f030f43cd80f1c6b","type":"link","url":"https://cs.stanford.edu/people/karpathy/advice.html","x":-2800,"y":270,"width":700,"height":330},{"id":"bed5bde0fd9f5c97","type":"text","text":"- General:\n\t- All-nighters are not worth it;\n\t\t- Sleep >>>\n\t\t- Try to maximize the # of nights your brain gets with the material\n\t- Attend tutorials or review sessions;\n\t\t- Just thinking about the material is already great\n- To prepare for the test\n\t-  Create a schedule of study\n\t\t- Write down bullet points of everything you need to know, and try to time-bound it so that you don't spend all the time in the beginning\n\t- Always try to look at previous tests BEFORE starting to study;\n\t- Reading and understanding IS NOT the same as replicating the content;\n\t\t- Make sure that you can actually write down the most important bits, and that you can re-derive them at will.\n\t- Always try to collaborate with others, but near the end (study alone first);\n\t- Don't only hang out only with stronger students;\n\t- Go to the prof before final exam at least once for office hours;\n\t- Study well in advance\n\t\t- ~3 days for midterms, ~6 days for exams\n\t- If things are going badly and you get too tired, in emergency situations, jug an energy drink.\n\t\t- They work. It's just chemistry.\n\t- For things like math: Exercise > Reading\n\t- Make yourself cheat sheet.\n\t- Study in places where other people study as well, even if not the same thing.\n\t\t- Makes you feel bad when you're the one that's not studying\n- On the day of the test\n\t- Optimal eating/drinking habit is: T-2 hours get coffee and food.\n\t- Study very intensely RIGHT before the test.\n\t\t- Short term memory is a wonderful thing, don't waste it!\n- During the test\n\t- Always use pencil for tests (to erase your garbage \"solutions\")\n\t- Look over all questions very briefly before start.\n\t- On test, do easy questions first.\n\t\t- Some questions somehow become much easier once you're \"warmed up\", I can't explain it.\n\t- Always try to be neat on the test.\n\t\t- A sad human being gives low marks.\n\t- Always BOX IN/CIRCLE the answer\n\t\t- Get in the mindset of a marker.\n\t- NEVER. EVER. EVER. Leave test early.\n\t\t- This is a clear example of a situation where potential benefits completely outweigh the cost.\n\t- Communicate with the marker.\n\t\t- Show the marker that you know more than what you put down.\n\t- Consider number of points per question (prioritize).\n\t- If there are <5 minutes left and you are still stuck on some question, STOP.\n\t\t- Your time is better spent re-reading all questions and making absolutely sure you did not miss any secondary questions, and that you answered everything.\n- Final note:\n\t- No one will care about your grades, unless they are bad.\n\t- Your time is a precious, limited resource.\n\t- Get to a point where you don't screw up on a test and then switch your attention to much more important endeavors.\n\t- Value recommendation letters from well-known professors.\n\t- Other than research projects, get involved with some group of people on side projects or better, start your own from scratch. Contribute to Open Source, make/improve a library. Get out there and create (or help create) something cool. Document it well. Blog about it. These are the things people will care about a few years down the road. Your grades? They are an annoyance you have to deal with along the way. Use your time well and good luck.","x":-2800,"y":650,"width":700,"height":1386},{"id":"322e4c52f574d14d","type":"text","text":"- The \"cheating\" method:\n    - Kid tried writing cheat sheet on his arm, ran out of room, rewrote multiple times\n    - Ended up memorizing everything: \"I hid it all in my head! The professor didn't even know I was cheating\"\n- Study socializing:\n    - Guy went to study groups to hang out with attractive people\n    - Had to learn material well enough to help/impress them → met his wife this way\n- The prediction game during lectures:\n    - Try to predict what professor will say/write next\n    - Keeps you engaged, feels like discovering it yourself\n    - Works better than note-taking for some people\n- Note-taking isn't universal:\n    - Some brains blank out when taking notes\n    - 100% attention + 0% notes works better for certain people\n    - Writing can actually distract from understanding\n- Teaching = actual learning:\n    - You only realize what you don't know when explaining to someone else\n    - Helping weaker students forces true understanding\n- Interest trumps everything:\n    - \"My brain finding interesting topic? Impossible to avoid ingesting all knowledge\"\n    - Can't force learning what doesn't interest you\n    - Makes school brutal but real life easier\n- Study environments vary wildly:\n    - Quiet libraries make some people zone out\n    - Busy coffee shops + headphones = deep focus for others\n    - Standard \"quiet place\" advice makes many feel like failures\n- The consistency principle:\n    - Working 10% more each day compounds massively over lifetime\n    - Small daily steps > occasional heroic efforts\n    - Regularity beats intelligence\n- Nobody teaches HOW to study:\n    - High school too easy to develop actual study skills\n    - University hits and you're screwed\n    - \"Learning to learn\" should be taught explicitly\n- The university problem:\n    - Zero agency, just following arbitrary rules\n    - Low stakes (nobody cares) + high stakes (future) = cynicism\n    - \"It wasn't all just a game anymore\" only happens after graduation\n- **Sleep vs cramming nuance:**\n    - Brain commits memories during sleep, solve problems easier next morning\n    - BUT if you've never done that problem type, cramming beats sleeping\n- Coffee timing:\n    - T-2 hours before: coffee + food ✓\n    - Right before test or during stress: always bad ✗\n- Grading curves create bad incentives:\n    - Some schools curve 50% → 75%\n    - Students game which courses to take instead of learning\n- Office hours dilemma:\n    - Feels manipulative to go without real questions\n    - Professors spot BS, but people who do it succeed more anyway\n- Stop overthinking methods:\n    - People spend 90% time reading about learning, 10% practicing\n    - Real formula: read/listen → apply/question, repeat\n    - Most \"learning methods\" are pseudo-science\n- All-nighters paradox:\n    - Bad for grades, great for bonding\n    - \"Made my best friends through all-nighters\"\n- Never leave tests early:\n    - You'll always find mistakes if you check again\n    - Zero cost to staying, massive upside","x":-3500,"y":650,"width":600,"height":1452},{"id":"353d4b0ee6388e23","type":"text","text":"# For students","x":-4200,"y":100,"width":600,"height":90,"color":"4"},{"id":"5ca11a74e4c8118e","type":"text","text":"# The higher education system is broken\n\nA working collection of problems with the current system, research, and ideas for how it could be better.\n\nBy [Rodrigo Dias](https://rgo.pt/).","x":-1650,"y":100,"width":700,"height":210,"color":"2"},{"id":"4cad20a33bfb10d0","type":"text","text":"This canvas contains the **🚧 WIP** collection of research, ideas, and problems behind the New Higher Education System. It is not a finished proposal or an active pilot.","x":-1650,"y":340,"width":700,"height":160,"color":"3"},{"id":"dc289ba7f11020cd","type":"text","text":"## What are grades even measuring?\n\nHow much you learned? When? At the end of the semester or during it? How much you know? Compared with whom? The average?","x":50,"y":100,"width":700,"height":140,"color":"1"},{"id":"0975a4fb1a3863fb","type":"text","text":"## What even are grades?\n\n- What are they measuring?\n\t- How good you become at that specific subject by the end?\n\t- How good you are during the semester?\n\t- How much you can build with it?\n\t- How much you know?\n\t- How many concepts you understand?\n- In comparison to **what**?\n\t- The average?\n\t- The median?\n\t- A 60% grade as a baseline?\n\t- Does it depend on the students in the current year, or on every student since the subject began? Can it change drastically?\n\nThe answer is that it unfortunately depends on the subject, the teacher, and...\n\n> [!question]\n> The original sentence ends here. What else should complete this list?","x":50,"y":390,"width":800,"height":461},{"id":"558743d14da33de0","type":"text","text":"## Human-made methods, notation, and models\n\nSometimes we are made to learn something created by other error-prone humans who definitely did not think of every possible way to solve a problem. It is then taught as if it were a law of physics.\n\nEven physics notation was made by fallible humans. There may be better notation or better ways to express the same ideas. There could even be mistakes in modern physical laws that we have not encountered yet.\n\nIt was God who made the underlying reality, not us humans. We are doing our best to understand it well enough to build bridges and do useful things, but it is often presented as exact and infallible.","x":900,"y":100,"width":750,"height":325},{"id":"5f5d21fce2833e11","type":"text","x":2600,"y":3040,"width":1300,"height":150,"text":"## Degree programs themselves can also be badly designed\n\nThis is a separate problem from students choosing the wrong degree for themselves.","color":"6"}],"edges":[{"id":"7b575b13be466b7a","fromNode":"5ca11a74e4c8118e","toNode":"4cad20a33bfb10d0","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top"},{"id":"9cd94f22e1832f56","fromNode":"5ca11a74e4c8118e","toNode":"dc289ba7f11020cd","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"right","toSide":"left","label":"core question","color":"3"},{"id":"70df43dd0fec12a5","fromNode":"dc289ba7f11020cd","toNode":"0975a4fb1a3863fb","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top"},{"id":"3c37409f1a3a7995","fromNode":"4cad20a33bfb10d0","toNode":"6d5efdb323892f0d","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"diagnosis","color":"1"},{"id":"09377a7d7585b0c2","fromNode":"6a5da23545c39676","toNode":"dc9c35e7bb73bb62","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"right","toSide":"left","label":"current roles","color":"5"},{"id":"5797bda3f5511d6b","fromNode":"dc9c35e7bb73bb62","toNode":"0892404cb36611fa","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"right","toSide":"left","label":"reform","color":"4"},{"id":"c2dae04ea443ea46","fromNode":"0892404cb36611fa","toNode":"4d10a5a670cbf6cb","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"test it","color":"4"},{"id":"6c4d74fe27154ed9","fromNode":"dc9c35e7bb73bb62","toNode":"ac9816c70d0b5369","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"learning","color":"5"},{"id":"0337e0a30747f7e5","fromNode":"dc9c35e7bb73bb62","toNode":"e40b0f5ed7229998","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"jobs","color":"5"},{"id":"f911752a19fdbc6e","fromNode":"dc9c35e7bb73bb62","toNode":"cba99104a7a73702","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"filtering","color":"5"},{"id":"efe87d3aaef28052","fromNode":"dc9c35e7bb73bb62","toNode":"e9a750c69b8663e5","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"maturation","color":"5"},{"id":"1848d9d34659e638","fromNode":"dc9c35e7bb73bb62","toNode":"8f8689ee64149323","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"social life","color":"5"},{"id":"90eaad0f7f9f701a","fromNode":"dc9c35e7bb73bb62","toNode":"97512e905ca4f89a","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"insurance","color":"5"},{"id":"164a985c020d516d","fromNode":"dc9c35e7bb73bb62","toNode":"c3e69b85cc590897","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"research","color":"5"},{"id":"eeca01855c1f51c0","fromNode":"dc9c35e7bb73bb62","toNode":"fd949e63862715b6","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"independence","color":"5"},{"id":"43be2a2916eef663","fromNode":"6d5efdb323892f0d","toNode":"6a282353f4d6f81e","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"1"},{"id":"e20717eba0d963e9","fromNode":"6a282353f4d6f81e","toNode":"40208c834c2bd7ab","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"1"},{"id":"b8979fa9a0712ac4","fromNode":"6a282353f4d6f81e","toNode":"6237f28886640a26","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"1"},{"id":"1f7aa834bce448c8","fromNode":"6a282353f4d6f81e","toNode":"c9b408acb26e2f74","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"1"},{"id":"736e985c05066218","fromNode":"6d5efdb323892f0d","toNode":"4452f0411febc1c8","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"1"},{"id":"6227f68fbf414b91","fromNode":"4452f0411febc1c8","toNode":"206595ff6f08b7f0","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"1"},{"id":"6bbfc21c2d373ee2","fromNode":"4452f0411febc1c8","toNode":"1cd66df557247172","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"1"},{"id":"178b577a9ddedf27","fromNode":"4452f0411febc1c8","toNode":"b04d9431185f455b","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"1"},{"id":"2624a8efdfaec9a3","fromNode":"4452f0411febc1c8","toNode":"2557aee20da9a019","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"1"},{"id":"b9dc7447141bd169","fromNode":"4452f0411febc1c8","toNode":"6c401365341b90ad","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"1"},{"id":"85205bc93d85dd79","fromNode":"6d5efdb323892f0d","toNode":"cab7cbd8a199ca1b","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"1"},{"id":"314af358af72f2e7","fromNode":"cab7cbd8a199ca1b","toNode":"f2d74526e5c2ed3f","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"1"},{"id":"45f344e146183428","fromNode":"cab7cbd8a199ca1b","toNode":"996851582ccb4b82","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"1"},{"id":"4e5965211d8d3916","fromNode":"cab7cbd8a199ca1b","toNode":"d0371e28c3de8ce8","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"1"},{"id":"c95d1a376c024f36","fromNode":"6d5efdb323892f0d","toNode":"1413bfb5fdc0520a","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"1"},{"id":"fe5ecf5c96d311a6","fromNode":"1413bfb5fdc0520a","toNode":"87c5f0e2b2765e28","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"1"},{"id":"1602b0d7b6607483","fromNode":"1413bfb5fdc0520a","toNode":"707c1a2bc3da8395","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"1"},{"id":"1a197c5bafef97d2","fromNode":"0892404cb36611fa","toNode":"0a17e9372dff2e35","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"4"},{"id":"7feb7e861d515fde","fromNode":"0892404cb36611fa","toNode":"11cceaf379455d0e","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"4"},{"id":"743f5ece292d8ee0","fromNode":"0892404cb36611fa","toNode":"52ffb2ebf9e212e2","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"4"},{"id":"aa95fba76f4fe860","fromNode":"0892404cb36611fa","toNode":"80e7e717b80302d7","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"4"},{"id":"5322eb338ff33830","fromNode":"0892404cb36611fa","toNode":"bcd970b900946a35","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"4"},{"id":"402cb64f87e4354b","fromNode":"0892404cb36611fa","toNode":"7b6b27050b31f4b7","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"4"},{"id":"97c79a833f4445f1","fromNode":"0892404cb36611fa","toNode":"6d56844a3b0940b0","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"4"},{"id":"8a66374313b834f5","fromNode":"4d10a5a670cbf6cb","toNode":"be6c1b210a74e5a0","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"4"},{"id":"b13eb80db0933d94","fromNode":"4d10a5a670cbf6cb","toNode":"595f1943a3c3f8d9","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"4"},{"id":"681480224ed19b7f","fromNode":"4d10a5a670cbf6cb","toNode":"9f1b6a7e87d21e57","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"4"},{"id":"2fa57c85c641d1c9","fromNode":"4d10a5a670cbf6cb","toNode":"f772988de899c2ba","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"4"},{"id":"83b4bc8efda3d0d3","fromNode":"4d10a5a670cbf6cb","toNode":"f6b31bb680b5d267","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"4"},{"id":"9a36d808fae26f56","fromNode":"d3882d06cefb8d2c","toNode":"eb8a2d105577f479","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"right","toSide":"left","label":"context","color":"1"},{"id":"7071953cbd46f6df","fromNode":"1927eaf2ab638174","toNode":"a380775b697b6c29","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top"},{"id":"b88d64c35d485b76","fromNode":"3bf79dbd6331bbb9","toNode":"6e55420f45d07374","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top"},{"id":"5c6cc3fa4681843f","fromNode":"860439a9b2c07f06","toNode":"05528dd7812a1f58","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top"},{"id":"31f886e752ee4c07","fromNode":"dc5aa724b10d2949","toNode":"8eac15b120e5b65b","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top"},{"id":"8cea0638e21ea7ff","fromNode":"d2b672efd12aec05","toNode":"091346db70ff52cb","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top"},{"id":"dff91fc512567923","fromNode":"0e526d710cb0f989","toNode":"2f9ebfeb50ea7214","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top"},{"id":"27e8aa5b0e9f2787","fromNode":"9e52c7e780b711c4","toNode":"64b2b19fcb76139c","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"source","color":"6"},{"id":"0b40f36720ec94dd","fromNode":"64b2b19fcb76139c","toNode":"9e43197e4aea4fdd","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"right","toSide":"left","label":"notes","color":"6"},{"id":"e6fbf907e35e256e","fromNode":"fdbc92b6d2f67fc0","toNode":"95fa05c1ef37ed6d","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"source","color":"6"},{"id":"6c45d6dce36c5287","fromNode":"95fa05c1ef37ed6d","toNode":"6eda5f9dcb7017fa","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"right","toSide":"left","label":"notes","color":"6"},{"id":"05674c13ab6e8c7f","fromNode":"4e3ddea59cb58537","toNode":"fbf101ecb3140d67","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"source","color":"6"},{"id":"4bf08af67982eda1","fromNode":"fbf101ecb3140d67","toNode":"43c8896d02f29981","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"right","toSide":"left","label":"notes","color":"6"},{"id":"304b86f21992f5e9","fromNode":"642aee981335824b","toNode":"72c7b2f82975e03c","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"source","color":"6"},{"id":"1cac9552d4995751","fromNode":"72c7b2f82975e03c","toNode":"a7bb225664376221","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"right","toSide":"left","label":"notes","color":"6"},{"id":"706359abb06e7d7e","fromNode":"5e47b2d1b4ddd288","toNode":"43c8896d02f29981","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"6"},{"id":"f50d0dd9f49b3fb4","fromNode":"ffecdb8cf8377855","toNode":"005f24422aaddb04","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"source note","color":"6"},{"id":"55b67013947a356a","fromNode":"9eb697401f112b16","toNode":"005f24422aaddb04","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"source note","color":"6"},{"id":"42cfa247876ddbcd","fromNode":"347689399f21302a","toNode":"005f24422aaddb04","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"source note","color":"6"},{"id":"adb4226dd90628e4","fromNode":"f06a17c610a91a55","toNode":"005f24422aaddb04","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"source note","color":"6"},{"id":"705e0a8cec451f8d","fromNode":"36e3f5b83a229b92","toNode":"005f24422aaddb04","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"source note","color":"6"},{"id":"86cdcda9caf0249a","fromNode":"4c6801863bd002c9","toNode":"005f24422aaddb04","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","label":"source note","color":"6"},{"id":"1e899d5b9a1b0884","fromNode":"f532b52cefa34642","toNode":"005f24422aaddb04","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"bottom","toSide":"top","color":"6"},{"id":"224c184ea82ca6ee","fromNode":"7f7b3d2388d04292","toNode":"2ae928def8c5fd1d","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"right","toSide":"left","color":"6"},{"id":"2bb881a5488c9ca6","fromNode":"02e87a4914c19f5b","toNode":"2ae928def8c5fd1d","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"right","toSide":"left","color":"6"},{"id":"f06c7872996675b7","fromNode":"c2fabea0103da462","toNode":"2ae928def8c5fd1d","toEnd":"arrow","fromSide":"right","toSide":"left","color":"6"},{"id":"b9de7140c82fa1e6","fromNode":"1413bfb5fdc0520a","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"5f5d21fce2833e11","toSide":"top","toEnd":"arrow","color":"1"}]}